{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB12874 - MONDO_18437",
  "verbalization": "Quizartinib, a kinase inhibitor, is associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a shared pathway involving Idarubicin. The evidence suggests that Quizartinib may be repurposed for AML with Npm1 somatic mutations, as it is indicated for AML with T(9;11)(p22;q23) and AML with Maturation, both of which are clinically relevant subtypes of AML. Idarubicin, a chemotherapy agent, is indicated for these subtypes, forming a chain of indications that links Quizartinib to AML with Npm1 mutations. The core mechanism likely involves the inhibition of enzymes or pathways that are dysregulated in AML, as both Quizartinib and Idarubicin are known to target leukemia cells. The shared indication chain and the presence of a common target (Idarubicin) support a mechanistic link, though the exact molecular mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence is consistent with a repurposing scenario, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the therapeutic potential.",
